WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.668
Validation is also a resource-control mechanism.

2
00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:12.197
Untrusted input can influence how much memory the program allocates, how long it loops, how many records it stores, how deeply it recurses, and how

3
00:00:12.197 --> 00:00:13.721
much work it schedules.

4
00:00:13.721 --> 00:00:17.151
Reasonable limits should be part of the input contract.

5
00:00:17.151 --> 00:00:22.868
Set maximum lengths, item counts, nesting depth, allocation budgets, and processing limits before accepting data.

6
00:00:22.868 --> 00:00:30.491
A design that loads everything into memory may be fine for small trusted files but inappropriate for larger external inputs.

7
00:00:30.491 --> 00:00:34.303
Streaming, chunking, and early rejection can keep resource use predictable.

8
00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:36.208
Failure behavior should be controlled.

9
00:00:36.208 --> 00:00:43.831
When input is malformed, oversized, inconsistent, or unsupported, the program should return a clear failure result, clean up temporary resources,

10
00:00:43.831 --> 00:00:47.643
preserve object invariants, and avoid leaving partially trusted state behind.

11
00:00:47.643 --> 00:00:49.548
Error messages need different audiences.

12
00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:53.741
User-facing messages should be useful without revealing sensitive implementation details.

13
00:00:53.741 --> 00:01:01.745
Internal logs can include more diagnostic context, but they should still avoid unsafe logging practices such as recording unnecessary secrets or

14
00:01:01.745 --> 00:01:02.888
enormous raw values.

15
00:01:02.888 --> 00:01:05.556
Validation failures should not be exceptional mysteries.

16
00:01:05.556 --> 00:01:08.605
They are expected outcomes at a trust boundary.

17
00:01:08.605 --> 00:01:15.847
Code should handle them through documented paths, and monitoring should distinguish normal invalid input from unusual patterns that deserve

18
00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:16.609
operational attention.

19
00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:19.277
The goal is predictable behavior under pressure.

20
00:01:19.277 --> 00:01:27.662
When limits are explicit, invalid or oversized input consumes bounded memory and time, cleanup remains reliable, and the program keeps its internal

21
00:01:27.662 --> 00:01:28.425
assumptions intact.

22
00:01:28.425 --> 00:01:31.855
Resource policies should be reviewed with operations in mind.

23
00:01:31.855 --> 00:01:40.240
A limit should have a reason, a failure response, and a signal that maintainers can observe when legitimate workloads approach that limit.
